INT. MATTRESS WAREHOUSE - MAILROOM - DAY

Stacks of boxed queens and kings reach the rafters. A conveyor rattles. Fluorescent light buzzes over steel shelving.

THE INTERN (19, stiff new polo, name tag blank) stands in the doorway holding a manila envelope like a live fish.

MS. PRUDENCE KALLIS (50s, cardigan, reading glasses on a beaded chain) sorts parcels at the counter. ANDRZEJ NOWICKI-FAYE (40s, forklift gloves tucked in his belt) sits on a pallet, eating a sandwich in precise bites.

Nobody speaks. The conveyor rattles.

THE INTERN
Um. Hi. I was told to report here.

PRUDENCE
By whom?

THE INTERN
A man in a tie.

PRUDENCE
They all wear ties.

She does not look up. Andrzej chews. The Intern steps into the room and sets the envelope on the counter.

THE INTERN
It says to give you this.

Prudence slits it open with a letter opener. Reads. Her mouth thins.

PRUDENCE
Roscoe.

THE INTERN
Sorry?

PRUDENCE
It says here your name is Roscoe.

ROSCOE
(startled)
Oh. Yes. Roscoe. Hi.

ANDRZEJ
Roscoe.

PRUDENCE
Roscoe, welcome to the mailroom.

ROSCOE
Thanks. What do I do?

PRUDENCE
You stand.

ROSCOE
Stand?

ANDRZEJ
You stand. You wait. You deliver nothing.

ROSCOE
That's it?

PRUDENCE
Seventeen years, and that is the entire job.

Andrzej folds his sandwich paper into a perfect square. Prudence slides the envelope into a drawer already full of identical ones. Roscoe adjusts his name tag, which still says nothing. The conveyor rattles on.

ROSCOE
Is there a handbook?

ANDRZEJ
There's a mattress.

He points. In the corner, between two stacks of boxes, a single twin bed made up with a grey blanket. A small nest of mail beside it.

ROSCOE
Does someone sleep here?

PRUDENCE
Not tonight.
